00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.620
Most of us probably think of malaria as
a disease confined to the tropics. It

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.130
thrives in Southeast Asia, the eastern
Mediterranean, parts of Central and South

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.320
America, and Africa which carries most of
the global malaria burden. And when we

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.220
think of malaria we probably think of
humid jungles or tropical lakes in

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.949
equatorial regions. As of 2017, 87
countries had ongoing malaria

00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:45.000
transmission, with places like the US and
Europe appearing to be safely off the

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.620
list. It's easy to assume that's because
the US and Europe are not tropical

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.049
places. But in fact, both places used to
be riddled with malaria. In the U.S. in

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:59.879
particular, it debilitated towns, caused
thousands of deaths, and even determined

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.589
the settlement patterns of the country.
But now it has been so thoroughly erased

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.390
that many people don't even know it used
to exist there at all. So how is it that

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.979
malaria once thrived in the US, and why
did it disappear? And perhaps most

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:17.969
importantly, can the methods of its
elimination in the US be copied

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:23.039
elsewhere? To understand the spread of
malaria in the US it's important to

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:27.060
consider the life cycle and behavior of
the Plasmodium parasite and it's vector,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.770
the mosquito. Malarial parasites are
carried by the Anopheles mosquito which

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.479
breeds in still water such as marshy
ponds and swamps, features that exist in

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:39.899
great numbers in the eastern and
southern United States. When a mosquito

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:44.460
bites an infected person a small amount
of blood is taken in which contains

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.990
microscopic malaria parasites, which then
mate in the gut of the mosquito and begin

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.189
a cycle of growth and multiplication.
About one week later, a form of the

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:56.490
parasite called a sporozoite
migrates to the mosquitos salivary

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.499
glands. When the mosquito takes its next
blood meal these parasites mix with the

00:02:01.499 --> 00:02:05.290
mosquitos saliva and are injected into
the next person being bitten.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.729
The sporozoites then rides in the
bloodstream towards the human liver. Once

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:14.830
it arrives, it enters a liver cell. There
it undergoes many rounds of division and

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.730
multiplication. A single infected liver
cell can lead to the creation of

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.840
thousands of new parasites. These new
parasites then migrate to infect red

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.340
blood cells where they can hide from the
body's immune system. Here they consume

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.230
the contents of the red blood cell and
divide to create even more parasites.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.930
Eventually the red blood cell they are
inhabiting ruptures and the new

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.340
parasites called merozoite are released.
These continue the cycle by invading

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.410
other red blood cells which subsequently
also rupture. The parasites living in the

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.980
bloodstream is what causes the symptoms
of malaria, which can range from

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.330
headaches, to fever, to seizures, to death
if the parasites block arteries in the

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.380
brain and kidneys. At this point the
parasite within the human bloodstream

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:00.480
can then be ingested once again by a
mosquito and transmitted to another

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:08.320
unsuspecting human and the cycle begins
again. No one knows for sure when malaria

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.220
was first introduced into humans but it
is thought to have been in prehistoric

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.840
times. But as for the US its
infestation with the disease was not

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.470
brought about until the 17th century
with the first arrival of slave ships

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:25.030
from Africa. Once the parasite was
introduced to North American shores

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.320
places like the wet low-lying plains of
Virginia and South Carolina became

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.940
overrun with the disease. The Carolinas
were initially thought of as a land of

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.350
paradise by European settlers but with
the introduction of malaria became known

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:43.330
as a ghastly place to live. An English
proverb at the time said "those who want

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:48.410
to die quickly, go to Carolina".
Incoming immigrants thus labeled certain

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.550
colonies as healthy and others as
dangerous. The Caribbean was understood

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.480
to be the most dangerous, with Florida
and the Carolinas being a close second.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.340
The Chesapeake was a bit better but only
in the northern colonies - New York, New

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.380
England, and Pennsylvania, did European
settlers thrive. And because most African

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.640
slaves had some tolerance to malaria and
white indentured laborers from Europe

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.440
did not, demand for slave labor in
malarious areas also increased. Malaria,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.640
along with other diseases like yellow
fever, thus had a substantial role in

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.940
determining settlement and labor
patterns in the colonies - patterns that

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.500
would eventually lead to the Civil War.
Malaria wreaked havoc in America for

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.230
centuries, and by the time of the first world
war,

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:34.760
malaria was a huge problem, especially
for the military, where men training in

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.860
the south were picking up the disease in
rapid numbers. 10,500 admissions for

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:45.260
malaria were reported from April 1917
through December 1919, involving a loss

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:49.610
of 130,000 training days. By the 1930s malaria had

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.540
become concentrated in 13 of the
country's southeastern states and there

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.740
were well over a million cases during
the Great Depression. Malaria had become

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:07.070
a major national problem. By 1933, malaria
deaths in America reached a new peak.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.000
Malaria thrives when poverty is high
where people lack access to adequate

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.690
health care and nutrition, and America
had just descended into the Great

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:19.220
Depression. But for the next decade where
malaria should have boomed, it instead

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.200
retreated. But the exact reason why isn't
as clear as you might think. To this day

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.220
scientists and historians fiercely argue
about which of the many factors was the

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.419
key to its eradication. At the time there
were two camps of opinion on the best

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:37.220
way to get rid of malaria. The first, to
attack the parasite inside the human

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:42.650
body. The second, to eliminate the vector
for the disease, the mosquito. If it were

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:46.310
possible to treat all members of a
community at once and eradicate the

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.450
parasite within them, then malaria could
be wiped out, even if the adult

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:54.100
mosquitoes carry on biting everyone. For
hundreds of years

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.180
people were aware that quinine obtained
from cinchona bark could be used to

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.610
alleviate the symptoms of and even
prevent malaria. So in 1916 scientists

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.720
carried out a study on 500,000 people in
Bolivar County Mississippi,

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:11.680
a place plagued with malaria, to see if
quinine could be used as an effective

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.420
treatment. They gave out doses of quinine
for free and indeed found that it

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:21.370
reduced malaria infections by 90%.
However even though high doses of

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.630
quinine were good for quickly ending an
episode of fever and chills they found

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:29.380
that people would not take enough
quinine on a regular basis to prevent

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.520
infection long term because of its bad
side effects.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:37.600
Therefore, it was useful for interrupting
and infection and relieving symptoms but

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.590
more often than not the infection would
just come right back. Thus quinine

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.430
distributed in large numbers definitely
helped but it would not be the complete

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.880
answer to getting rid of malaria in
America. It would take more than

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.440
medicating the population to get rid of
this persistent disease. Once it became

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.490
known that the mosquito was the cause of
malaria many people believed its death

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.390
would be the way to eliminate the
disease. But killing the adult stage of

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:05.710
the mosquito is challenging since they
infest the world in 3d space and at the

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:10.780
time there wasn't any reliable methods
for doing so. Therefore many believed it

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.930
was best to attack the mosquito at the
most vulnerable point in its lifecycle -

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:19.420
the larval stage. Mosquitoes lay their
eggs in marshy stagnant water which then

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:23.080
hatch into larvae which will eventually
develop into full-grown malaria

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.430
spreading mosquitoes. And so one way to
hit them where it hurts is to eliminate

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:31.600
their breeding grounds, the marshy
stagnant water. And unluckily for the

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.710
mosquitoes the 1930s brought a wave of
public works projects intended to boost

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.300
the economy, including malaria control and
lots and lots of digging. The Works

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:45.040
Progress Administration put people to
work digging 32,000 miles of ditches and

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:51.100
draining 623,000 watered acres. And when
draining wasn't possible, coating the

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:54.820
surfaces of ponds with oil and spraying
their habitats with a compound called

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:59.440
Paris green further smothered the larvae.
These efforts coincided with a sharp

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:04.900
decline in malaria transmission in the
1930s, but by 1940 while less common than

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.220
it once was,
malaria still persisted.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:12.290
During this time the US army still
trained its men in many areas of the

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:17.210
Southeast US where malaria still had its
grip. Not wanting to repeat the hard

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.350
lessons from the First World War,
large-scale anti malaria operations were

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:27.260
undertaken. 40,000 acres of surface water
was eliminated 4.7 million gallons of

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:31.760
diesel oil larvacide was used, and 9.8
million dollars was spent on this

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:37.250
all-out war against malaria. Then in 1944
one of the most effective mosquito

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:44.270
killers of all time was invented - DDT.
Thanks in part to DDT by 1945 malaria

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:48.200
transmission in the US had dropped
significantly and the disease's days in

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:53.330
the US were numbered.
Then in 1946, the CDC was born, with the

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:57.560
primary mission of finally getting rid
of malaria in America once and for all.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:03.020
During the CDC's first few years more
than 6.5 million homes were sprayed with

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:07.970
DDT. This along with even more wetland
drainage pushed the disease out of

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:13.790
existence. By 1951 malaria was considered
eliminated altogether from the country.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:19.520
The onslaught of DDT, drainage works,
habitat oiling, and preventive medication

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.110
had finally worked. However there are
dozens of other factors which also

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:28.310
contributed to malaria's death in
America. Some historians firmly believe

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:32.360
that the key factor in its eradication
was actually population movement away

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.650
from rural areas, while others think it
was simply better education about the

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:40.640
disease that did the trick. Others think
it was general economic improvement and

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.630
the installation of screens on houses,
and yet others think it was actually a

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:49.160
massive drought that led to malaria's
demise. This multivariable attack on the

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.600
disease and the uncertainty that that
causes has left scientists unsure of how

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:58.310
to best translate these results to other
parts of the world today. This, along with

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:02.210
the fact that many of the approaches
taken in America cannot or should not be

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:06.260
taken in other parts of the world has
made eliminating malaria globally a

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:11.270
massive, still unsolved problem. Most can
agree that draining marshy areas in

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:16.650
America helped lead to malaria's decline
in some amount. However, in Africa where

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:20.610
most of the world's cases of malaria
occur today, such methods are not

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:24.540
feasible, because the mosquitoes there
breed in small pools of water that

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:28.680
form from rainfall, spread across the
landscape. It is difficult if not

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.450
impossible to predict when and where the
breeding sites will form and to find and

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.790
treat them before the adult mosquitoes
emerge.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:40.800
DDT too was instrumental in eliminating
malaria in America since it's so

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:44.910
effective at killing mosquitoes. The
problem is we now know it's also great

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.840
at killing everything else. It was banned
pretty much everywhere in the world in

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.790
the 1970s and 80s, and although it's
still being used in some places

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:58.200
scientists urged it to be used as a last
resort to combat malaria. Distributing

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.920
anti malaria medicine also would
certainly help if everyone vulnerable to

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.210
the disease had plentiful access to it
but the cost of this can be prohibitive

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:10.620
and while some progress can be made with
this method the geography of places like

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:14.790
Africa make it so even if you make gains
in one area, the disease will just pop

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:19.170
back up again as it returns from a
surrounding area. These problems along

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:23.460
with new drug and insecticide resistance
within the parasite and mosquito makes

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.490
it so new approaches must be taken in
this global battle.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:31.200
Luckily organizations like the Bill and
Melinda Gates Foundation are working

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.860
towards just that putting their
resources in large part towards better

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.910
data collection along with research and
development for creating new medicines

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:43.560
and vaccines. And recently scientists
have been experimenting with genetically

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:47.700
modified mosquitoes intended to
drastically reduce mosquito populations

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.030
in the wild. It's a drastic new
development in the battle against

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:55.110
mosquito-borne illness and is something
we'll cover in depth in a future video.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:59.330
No single strategy to combat malaria
will ever be effective everywhere and

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:03.990
unfortunately there's no silver bullet
for this centuries-old problem. But with

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:08.490
long term commitment and a flexible
strategy along with much more funding it

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.790
may be possible to eliminate malaria
everywhere in the world

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:16.310
one day soon.
Malaria was in part wiped out in America

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:20.779
because of effective military strategy - a
strategy that took thousands of

00:12:20.779 --> 00:12:25.820
man-hours and millions of dollars and at
the time seemed impossible. There have

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.990
been many unlikely achievements like
this throughout history, from malaria and

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:33.860
yellow fever eradication in different
parts of the world, to the building of

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:38.870
the Panama Canal, to the successful
invasion of Normandy on D-Day, all things

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:43.100
which took unprecedented levels of
coordination and strategy, and all things

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:47.389
that easily could have failed with the
wrong plans set in motion. You can learn

00:12:47.389 --> 00:12:51.410
